{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. h4 g4 5. Ne5 Nf6 6. d4 d6 7. Nd3 Nxe4 8. Bxf4 Nc6 9. Nc3 f5 10. Be2 Bg7 { Laboratory starts here. Nf3 prevents an immediate queen check on h4. Black uses ...g5 to keep f4, so h4 attacks the supporting chain. After ...g4 the knight must move; Ne5 takes an active central post. Develop Bc4 or d4 according to Black's reply, then solve king safety. The h-file may open for either side; do not assume the rook alone creates an attack. } 11. Nxe4 { Candidate comparison. The knight captures a knight on e4. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 11... fxe4 12. Bg5 Bf6 13. Nf2 Bxg5 14. hxg5 Qxg5 15. Nxe4 { Candidate comparison. The knight captures a pawn on e4. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 15... Qg6 16. Ng3 Bd7 17. Bd3 Qe6+ 18. Qe2 Nxd4 19. Qxe6+ { Candidate comparison. The queen captures a queen on e6. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 19... Nxe6 20. Bxh7 Ng5 *

