{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. h4 g4 5. Ne5 d6 6. Nxg4 Nc6 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 9. d4 Bh6 10. Nd5 Qxd4 { Laboratory starts here. Black attacks Ne5 with the d-pawn, but the knight can capture the advanced g4 pawn while escaping. This concrete resource is stronger than mechanically retreating to d3 in this exact position. Develop the bishops and build the center while checking whether the knight will be attacked again. Compare Black's bishop development with attempts to preserve f4. } 11. Nxc7+ { Candidate comparison. The knight captures a pawn on c7. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 11... Kd8 12. Qxd4 Nxd4 13. Nxa8 Nxc2+ 14. Kf2 Nxa1 15. Bd3 { Candidate comparison. Places the bishop on d3. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 15... Rg8 16. Bd2 Rg3 17. Bb5 Nc2 18. Rc1 Ne3 19. Ba5+ { Candidate comparison. Gives check with the bishop on a5. The opponent must answer the check; inspect legal king moves, captures and blocks. } 19... b6 20. Nxb6 Rxg2+ *

