{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 d5 4. exd5 Nf6 5. d4 Nxd5 6. Bc4 Be6 7. Bxd5 Qxd5 8. O-O Nc6 9. Bxf4 O-O-O 10. Be3 Qh5 { Laboratory starts here. Black exchanges central pawns and develops toward the returned material. Bc4 creates pressure while White builds a useful d4 center. Castle, develop Nc3 and decide whether the f4 pawn is worth a separate tempo. A lead in development is valuable only if the pieces have targets. } 11. Nbd2 { Candidate comparison. Develops the queen knight while leaving the c-pawn free. The knight supports e4 and may seek c4 or a route through f1. } 11... Bd6 12. c3 Rhe8 13. Re1 f6 14. Bf2 Bf4 15. Nf1 { Candidate comparison. Places the knight on f1. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 15... g5 16. Qc2 Kb8 17. Bg3 Bxg3 18. hxg3 Qf7 19. b3 { Candidate comparison. Prepares a fianchetto and changes the long diagonal. Check the bishop and corner rook before assuming the next developing move is safe. } 19... Ne7 20. Rad1 Nf5 *

